  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The solvent is the substance present in the larger amount that dissolves the other; here it is water and the salt is the solute.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A saturated solution holds as much solute as it can at that temperature; any extra solute remains undissolved.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The syllabus definition is the mass of solute that saturates 100 g of water at a stated temperature.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    500 cm³ = 0.500 dm³, so c = 0.20 ÷ 0.500 = 0.40 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    n = 2.0 ÷ 40 = 0.050 mol in 0.250 dm³, giving 0.20 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    n = 0.50 × 0.200 = 0.100 mol, and 0.100 × 58.5 = 5.85 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Dilution conserves the moles; a tenfold increase in volume gives a tenfold fall in concentration.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    c₂ = c₁V₁ ÷ V₂ = (2.0 × 10.0) ÷ 100 = 0.20 M.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    V₂ = c₁V₁ ÷ c₂ = (4.0 × 50) ÷ 0.50 = 400 cm³, so the water added is 400 − 50 = 350 cm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Dissolving a gas is exothermic, so cooling the water increases its solubility; raising the pressure would also help.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    It now contains more solute than it can hold at 20 °C, so it is supersaturated until the excess crystallises.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    5.0 × 100 ÷ 250 = 2.0 g per 100 g of water.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so 250 cm³ = 250 ÷ 1000 = 0.250 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The solubility is per 100 g of water, so the saturated solution weighs 100 + 20 = 120 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    At 80 °C, 150 g of solution contains 50 g of salt and 100 g of water; cooling it allows only 20 g to stay dissolved, so 30 g crystallise.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Cooling a hot saturated solution gives crystals, which are then filtered, washed and dried; evaporating to dryness gives a contaminated powder.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Cold water dissolves surface impurities but does not significantly dissolve the product.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Heating drives off the water built into the crystal lattice, leaving the anhydrous salt.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    M = 120 + 126 = 246, and 126 ÷ 246 × 100 = 51 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Moles of salt = 3.20 ÷ 160 = 0.0200; moles of water = 1.80 ÷ 18 = 0.100; the ratio is 0.100 ÷ 0.0200 = 5, so x = 5, which is option D.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Sodium chloride is ionic and its ions are free to move in solution, so the solution conducts.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Polar water molecules attract the ions of the lattice and hydrate them, pulling them into solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    c = 0.10 ÷ 0.200 = 0.50 M.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Colligative properties depend on the number of dissolved particles, not on their identity, so colour is not among them.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Sodium chloride gives two moles of ions per mole, so for the same molarity it produces about twice as many particles.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Gas solubility falls as the temperature rises, which is why warm soda goes flat.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    M = 250, and the water contributes 90 g per 250 g, so 25.0 × 90 ÷ 250 = 9.0 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Saturation is a statement about the maximum solute at a stated temperature, not about concentration or conductivity.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A concentration of one mole per cubic decimetre is written 1 M, or 1 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
